// Hale stdlib — std::observe::Probes (GH #1477 S1).
//
// A main-locus param of this type asks the runtime to serve the
// process's health over HTTP on `port`:
//
//     params { probes: std::observe::Probes { port: 9090 }; }
//
// `/healthz` (503 naming a queue that holds work its consumer has not
// touched for the stall window), `/readyz` (200 once the main locus's
// births are done, 503 while it drains) and `/capacity` (each queue's
// waiting cells, capacity and stall time, as JSON). The probes answer
// from a thread of the runtime's own, not from a pool the program
// places, so a wedged pool or a main thread busy in its run() cannot
// keep them from answering: the param is declared and named like any
// other, and a placement does not apply to it. Its birth starts the
// listener; the main locus marks the process ready once every birth is
// done. The check allows one, on the main locus, built by its default:
// a construction of the main locus does not supply another.
locus __StdObserveProbes {
    params {
        port: Int = 9090;
    }
    birth() {
        std::process::__probes_start(self.port);
    }
}
